seeit has invited automotive journalists  to a driving safety training session in  Innsbruck to test the limits of both Car  and Driver in winter conditions  Sebastian Stott explains what drivers  have to watch out for he points out that  the first thing we need in snow is good  winter tires obviously there's no tires  on this station wagon are optimal if  they're new the tread is deep and the  traction good also important are the  wiper blades drivers should always check  the wiper blades because the streaks bad  ones leave on the windshield are not  only annoying they can seriously  impaired visibility and he simulates a  situation where the headlights are  covered with snow they have to be  cleaned up  not only so you can see the road and a  heavy nighttime snowfall but so other  drivers can see you and if the hood is  carrying a heavy load of snow it's a  good idea to clear that off as well so  it doesn't blow up onto the windshield  and hamper visibility the snow should  also be scraped off the roof so it won't  blow onto the cars following along  behind and obscure their vision let's  fill fires on position on forecast  anybody entire pan is fine  also very important is the proper seat  position so the driver feels safe  Sebastien conducts what he calls a risk  test he lays his wrist on top of the  steering wheel palms down and has to  make sure his shoulders don't pull away  from the back seat if they do he knows  he has to push the seat forward but this  time they don't so he's ready to go with  a 221 kilowatt engine under the hood and  all-wheel drive on the road it can be  lots of fun to drift across the ice in  this seeit station wagon and racing mode  the Cupra handles very nimbly all the  stability assistance systems can be  switched off  sebastien explains that he's deactivated  the electronic stability control this is  known as the fun mode but only for  tracks away from public roads as we can  see he only keeps one hand on the  steering wheel because it's harder to  maintain full control with both hands on  it especially when drifting on snow and  ice in tricky conditions like these  drivers should steer and accelerate very  cautiously in small doses and with a  sensitive touch but at this test site  Sebastian can just step on the gas and  tackle the snowy terrain head-on  yes any remarks that the Leon Cupra has  a Haldex clutch which means that the car  tends to operate like a front-wheel  drive but as soon as the front wheel  starts spinning the electronics register  the sudden loss of traction and  activates the rear wheel drive too that  gets the car moving quickly especially  in snow this special all-wheel drive  technology is quite impressive in mili  second increments it distributes the  power to the wheels that need it most  car buyers would do well to take the  opportunity to test their limits in  training sessions like this one they'll  learn how to react in dangerous  situations so they can stay safe on the  road and have plenty of fun doing it  [Music]  you
 

